statement:
Fix a universe $\mathcal U$ and use ordinary categories with $\mathcal U$-small Hom sets. All smallness below is relative to $\mathcal U$; object collections may belong to a larger universe. For an infinite regular cardinal $\kappa\in\mathcal U$, a category is $\kappa$-filtered if every diagram with fewer than $\kappa$ morphisms has a cocone. Regular means a union of fewer than $\kappa$ sets of size less than $\kappa$ has size less than $\kappa$. An object $P$ is $\kappa$-presentable if $\operatorname{Hom}(P,-)$ preserves small $\kappa$-filtered colimits. A category is accessible if, for some such $\kappa$, it has all small $\kappa$-filtered colimits and a small family of $\kappa$-presentable objects such that every object is a small $\kappa$-filtered colimit of that family. Colimits mean [def-weil-pbc-category-colimit].

A functor between accessible categories is accessible if it preserves small $\lambda$-filtered colimits for some infinite regular $\lambda$ for which the source has these colimits. The following constructions yield accessible categories and accessible projection functors:
(1) the product $\prod_{s\in S}\mathcal A_s$ of any small family of accessible categories, with its projections to the factors;
(2) for accessible functors $F,G:\mathcal A\to\mathcal B$, the inserter $\operatorname{Ins}(F,G)$, whose objects are $(X,u)$ with $u:FX\to GX$ and whose morphisms $(X,u)\to(Y,v)$ are arrows $f:X\to Y$ with $G(f)u=vF(f)$, with projection $(X,u)\mapsto X$;
(3) for accessible functors $F,G:\mathcal A\to\mathcal B$ and natural transformations $\alpha,\beta:F\Rightarrow G$, the equifier, namely the full subcategory of $\mathcal A$ on objects $X$ satisfying $\alpha_X=\beta_X$, with its inclusion into $\mathcal A$. No fixed common accessibility cardinal is asserted.

